    "content": "Banana Ball is a fast-paced, entertainment-first variation of baseball created by the Savannah Bananas designed to eliminate downtime and maximize fun. Its purpose is to engage fans through, showmanship, quicker games—capped at two hours—and high-energy rules, such as fans catching foul balls for outs, no bunting, and allowed stealing of first base. This video explains the key rules of Banana Ball:02:30The Savannah BananasYouTube• Feb 21, 2024 Key features of Banana Ball include: Fan Engagement: A foul ball caught in the stands is an out, directly involving fans in the game. Speed and Flow: Games are played within a strict two-hour time limit, and no walks are allowed; a \"ball four\" triggers a sprint where the hitter can advance as many bases as possible while the ball is thrown to every fielder. Point-Based System: Rather than total runs, games are won by points; each inning is worth one point, and the team with the most runs in that inning wins the point. Showmanship: Players perform choreographed dances, trick plays, and entertainment, similar to the Harlem Globetrotters. Rules Against Stalling: Batters cannot step out of the box, and bunting is strictly prohibited. Tie-Breaker: Tied games are decided by a \"showdown\" tiebreaker, where one hitter faces one pitcher. Banana Ball, created by Jesse Cole, serves to keep fans engaged and avoid the slow pace often associated with traditional baseball.",
